<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ceolwulf II of Mercia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 874–c. 879</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ceolwulf II was king of Mercia from 874 to about 879, installed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> after Burgred fled. In 877 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> divided Mercia, settling the eastern part and leaving him the west. Coins show that he issued a joint coinage with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>, and recently found hoards suggest a closer alliance than West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> sources admit.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ceolwulf-ii-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Ceolwulf II of Mercia" width="320" height="313" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG - Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ceolwulf_II,_King_of_Mercia,_silver_penny;_struck_874%E2%80%93circa_880_AD_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ceolwulf's name means ship-wolf. On the evidence of his name he is thought to belong to the C dynasty of Mercian kings, a family that claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pybba-of-mercia">Pybba of Mercia</a> and may have had ties to the ruling family of the Hwicce in south-west Mercia. His immediate ancestry is unknown, but he is thought to descend from Ceolwulf I of Mercia through his daughter Ælfflæd, who married first Wigmund, son of King Wiglaf, and then Beorhtfrith, son of King Beorhtwulf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> calls him an unwise king's thane who swore oaths and gave hostages to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a>, promising that the kingdom would be ready for them on whatever day they wanted it. The Chronicle was compiled on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>, Burgred's brother-in-law, and its account is considered biased, written to strengthen the claims of Alfred and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> to the overlordship of Mercia. Several thegns who had witnessed charters under Burgred witnessed them under Ceolwulf, and Mercian bishops witnessed his charters, which points to his acceptance in Mercia.</p><p>In 878 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodri-the-great">Rhodri Mawr</a> of Gwynedd was killed in battle against the English. Alfred was then occupied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> and Mercia traditionally claimed hegemony over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, so the English leader was probably Ceolwulf. In 881 Rhodri's sons defeated the Mercians at the Battle of the Conwy, which Welsh annals called the revenge of God for Rhodri; the Mercian leader by then was almost certainly Ceolwulf's successor, Æthelred. By 883 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-lord-of-the-mercians">Æthelred, Lord of the Mercians</a>, ruled Mercia with Alfred's support, and Ceolwulf is counted the last king of independent Mercia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Three types of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> penny were issued in Ceolwulf's name. Most were minted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and are of the Cross-and-Lozenge type that Alfred also used. Simon Keynes and the numismatist Mark Blackburn first proposed that Alfred alone was recognised in London in about 875, but a Cross-and-Lozenge penny of Ceolwulf that came to light in 1998 appears to be contemporary with Alfred's earliest coinage.</p><p>In 2015 a metal-detectorist found a hoard of coins, jewellery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> ingots near Watlington, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a>, dating to the late 870s, with thirteen of the rare Two Emperors pennies that show Alfred and Ceolwulf seated side by side. A find near Leominster of coins of about 879, presumed buried by retreating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>, also depicts both kings, and some experts read them as equals. Gareth Williams of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> said that these coins &quot;enable us to reinterpret our history at a key moment in the creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as a single kingdom.&quot;</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ceolwulf II of Mercia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Vikings installed <b>Ceolwulf II</b> and in 877 divided the kingdom, settling the eastern half, later part of the Danelaw, and leaving the west to him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
